@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-runners Test runners
@brief The runner initialises the framework and returns the exit code.

@details
A test program usually has one
@ref micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::runner "runner", a global object,
which `main()` uses in three steps:
1. `runner::initialise()` reads the command-line options, from `argc` and
   `argv` (see @ref micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cli); on platforms
   without a command line, the overload that takes the options,
   @ref micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::runner_options "runner_options",
   sets them directly. The call is optional; without it, the default
   options are used;
2. `runner::add_top_suite()` returns the top suite, on which `main()`
   creates its own test cases (see
   @ref micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-test-suites). The call is
   optional, for programs whose test cases are all in other suites;
3. @ref micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::runner::run_suites
   "runner::run_suites()" runs the other suites, writes the totals, and
   returns the exit code, which `main()` returns to the system.

Several runners may coexist, global or local, each with its own suites:
a runner runs only the suites that refer to it, so the runners do not
interfere (see @ref micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::runner "runner").

The exit code is:
- 0 when all the checks passed, when the test cases were only listed, or
  when the usage was displayed by `--help`;
- 1 when a check failed, or when the program used the runner wrongly (for
  example, it added no suite at all);
- 2 when the command line had an error, when the output file could not be
  opened, or when the filters selected no test case.

@par Aborting the session
@ref micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::runner::abort "runner::abort()" ends
the session immediately: the report is completed (with a `Bail out!` line
with the TAP reporter, in place of the totals), then the handler is called,
if there is one, and, if there is none, or if it returns, the process is
terminated with `::abort()`. A failed `require()` calls it in
the builds without exceptions (see
@ref micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-requirements).
@ref micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::runner::set_abort_handler
"runner::set_abort_handler()" sets the handler, which can, for example,
reset an embedded target or signal the result to a debugger; it returns the
previous handler, and `nullptr` restores the default behaviour. With
exceptions, the handler may throw, to return control to the application,
but the runner cannot be used afterwards.

@par Reading the session
These functions of the runner are useful to the code of `main()`:
- @ref micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::runner::totals "runner::totals()"
  returns the totals of the session, complete after `run_suites()`
  returned;
- @ref micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::runner::verbosity_level
  "runner::verbosity_level()" returns the verbosity of the console, which
  the tests may use to decide whether to display their own messages;
- @ref micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::runner::is_listing
  "runner::is_listing()" tells whether the test cases are only listed, when
  their bodies do not run;
- @ref micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::runner::application_arguments
  "runner::application_arguments()" returns the command-line arguments left
  to the application.

@par Example

@code{.cpp}
#include <micro-os-plus/micro-test-plus.h>

namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

mt::runner tr;

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  tr.initialise (argc, argv);

  auto& ts = tr.add_top_suite ("Minimal");

  ts.test ("Check truth", [] (auto& t) {
    t.expect (true);
  });

  return tr.run_suites ();
}
@endcode

The program writes (the passed checks are displayed only with the
`--verbose` option):

@code{.console}
# Running: minimal
# Built with ...
TAP version 14
# Subtest: Minimal
    ok 1 - Check truth # { passed, 1 check }

    1..1
ok 1 - Minimal # { passed, 1 check in 1 test case, time: X.XXX ms }

1..1
# { total: 1 check passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 1 test suite, time: X.XXX ms }
@endcode

The name of the top suite is optional. Without it, the suite is named
after the program, the file name of `argv[0]` without its extension
(`minimal` above); without `argv[0]`, it is named `default suite`.

@par Without a command line
On the platforms that have no command line, such as many embedded
targets, `main()` defines the options with `runner_options`, starting from
the defaults, and passes them to `initialise()`:

@code{.cpp}
#include <micro-os-plus/micro-test-plus.h>

namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

mt::runner tr;

int
main (void)
{
  mt::runner_options options{};
  options.selected_reporter = mt::reporter_kind::human;
  options.verbosity_level = mt::verbosity::verbose;

  tr.initialise (options);

  auto& ts = tr.add_top_suite ("Embedded");

  ts.test ("Check truth", [] (auto& t) {
    t.expect (mt::eq (6 * 7, 42));
  });

  return tr.run_suites ();
}
@endcode

The members of `runner_options` are the options of the command line (see
@ref micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cli): the reporter, the verbosity, the
colours, the output file, the filter patterns, the listing of the test
cases, and the display of the usage.
